From 65a787d7a3b3882780a6da18c39b5702fe3d5a8a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Raza <raza@bukc.edu.pk>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 07:50:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(auth): two real gaps in the cross-tab session-replacement
 guard
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The previous fix (session-refresh race) only covered ONE of the paths a
silent identity swap can happen through, and had a genuine false-positive
bug on top. Both confirmed and fixed.

1. FALSE POSITIVE: the 'session replaced' banner was firing on a plain
   WRONG-PASSWORD login attempt, which makes no sense — a fresh login call
   never carries an access token in the first place, so a 401 from it means
   'bad credentials,' not 'your token expired.' The transparent refresh-
   and-retry logic in api() didn't distinguish this: ANY 401 from ANYWHERE
   (including /api/auth/login itself) triggered a silent refresh attempt
   using whatever cookie happened to be sitting in the browser — which,
   worse, could silently adopt a completely unrelated identity from another
   tab's session onto a page where the user hadn't even successfully logged
   in yet. Fixed: the retry-refresh path now only fires when the ORIGINAL
   request actually carried an access token (i.e. was a genuinely
   authenticated call that could plausibly have failed because that token
   expired) — login, register, forgot-password, and every other
   unauthenticated endpoint are now correctly exempt.

2. STILL-OPEN GAP: the mismatch check only ran on the 401-retry path, using
   an in-memory 'who am I' variable that resets to nothing on every page
   reload. That meant a RELOAD had nothing to compare the refreshed
   identity against, and would silently accept whatever the (possibly
   since-overwritten-by-another-tab) cookie said — exactly the reported
   'student tab refresh shows the admin panel' symptom. Fixed: the tab's
   believed identity now persists in sessionStorage (survives a reload,
   but — unlike cookies/localStorage — stays genuinely isolated per tab),
   and refreshSession() is now the single function both the app-load
   bootstrap AND the 401-retry path call, so the mismatch check applies
   uniformly everywhere a refresh can happen, not just one of the two
   places it needs to.

This also explains the reported EQUIP-AVAIL-05 stock-total leak on a
Student's equipment card ('5 available of 6') without any separate fix
being needed there: the client's showTotal={isStaff} gating and the
server's staff-only totalStock field were both already correct (confirmed
by reading both, and by existing passing tests) — the leak was a symptom
of the tab's *actual* authenticated identity silently being staff at that
moment, not a flaw in the visibility rule itself.

Also spent this session verifying EQUIP-AVAIL-04/11/12/13/19 (the T-24hr
equipment lock mechanism) against real simulated event/booking data —
all five already correctly implemented and already had solid existing
test coverage (T-807/T-808 etc. in equipment-allocation.test.ts) from
earlier work, no code changes needed there. Ran a full live scenario by
hand first: equipment_lock_at computed as exactly T-24h before the event
(0.0 minute drift) and the allocation stays unlocked/fully available
until that boundary; moving the event's actual time to cross T-24h
correctly triggers the lock and subtracts the locked quantity from
available_units; a sufficiently-stocked lock produces zero notifications
(EQUIP-AVAIL-19, silence is confirmation); a deliberately understocked
one produces exactly one accurate T24_LOCK_ALERT regardless of any
borrower's own return deadline (EQUIP-AVAIL-13). Also confirmed
equipment_lock_at can't be tampered with directly — it's recomputed from
the session's own slot by a trigger on every write, by design.

Verified: tsc --noEmit clean, production build clean, full server suite
(all 7 files, 156/156) passes unchanged.
---
 client/src/lib/api.ts | 91 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-------------
 1 file changed, 65 insertions(+), 26 deletions(-)

diff --git a/client/src/lib/api.ts b/client/src/lib/api.ts
index 5ca2cfd..44aa6e1 100644
--- a/client/src/lib/api.ts
+++ b/client/src/lib/api.ts
@@ -2,29 +2,46 @@
  * API client. Implements the confirmed auth model:
  *  - access token held in memory only (never localStorage — XSS-safe)
  *  - refresh token in an HTTP-only cookie (sent automatically with credentials)
- *  - on a 401, transparently try /api/auth/refresh once, then retry the request
+ *  - on a 401 from an AUTHENTICATED call, transparently try /api/auth/refresh
+ *    once, then retry the request
  *
  * Feature routers add typed wrappers over `api()`; this is the transport.
  *
  * Cross-tab caveat (by design of HTTP cookies, not fixable in app code): the
  * refresh cookie lives at the browser/origin level, not per-tab. If a Super
  * Admin is signed in on one tab and a Coordinator signs in on another tab of
- * the SAME browser, that second login overwrites the ONE shared cookie. The
- * first tab doesn't notice immediately (its access token is still valid in
- * memory), but the next time its access token expires and this module
- * silently refreshes on its behalf, the cookie now belongs to the OTHER
- * account — so the refresh would otherwise come back as the wrong identity
- * with no signal to the user. We can't make two simultaneous different
- * logins coexist in tabs of one browser (that would need separate cookie
- * jars — separate browser profiles / an Incognito window / a different
- * browser altogether), but we CAN detect the mismatch and force a clean,
- * explicit sign-out instead of silently swapping the dashboard underneath
- * the user. See SESSION_REPLACED_EVENT below.
+ * the SAME browser, that second login overwrites the ONE shared cookie. We
+ * can't make two simultaneous different logins coexist in tabs of one
+ * browser (that would need separate cookie jars — separate browser profiles
+ * / an Incognito window / a different browser altogether), but we CAN detect
+ * when a refresh comes back as a different identity than this tab itself
+ * believes it is, and force a clean, explicit sign-out instead of silently
+ * swapping the dashboard underneath the user. Two places this can surface,
+ * both guarded below:
+ *  1. Mid-session: this tab's access token expires, a request 401s, the
+ *     transparent retry silently refreshes — but the shared cookie now
+ *     belongs to whoever logged in on another tab since.
+ *  2. On reload: a plain in-memory "who am I" flag resets to nothing on
+ *     every page load, so a naive bootstrap refresh has nothing to compare
+ *     against and would silently accept whatever the (possibly since-
+ *     overwritten) cookie says. sessionStorage — unlike a JS module
+ *     variable — survives a reload but, unlike cookies/localStorage, is
+ *     genuinely isolated per tab, so it's exactly the right place to
+ *     remember "what THIS tab itself last confirmed being logged in as."
  */
 const API_BASE = import.meta.env.VITE_API_BASE ?? '';
+const TAB_USER_KEY = 'bukc:tabUserId';
 
 let accessToken: string | null = null;
-let currentUserId: string | null = null;
+let currentUserId: string | null = readTabUserId();
+
+function readTabUserId(): string | null {
+  try {
+    return sessionStorage.getItem(TAB_USER_KEY);
+  } catch {
+    return null; // sessionStorage can throw in some locked-down contexts
+  }
+}
 
 export function setAccessToken(token: string | null): void {
   accessToken = token;
@@ -33,9 +50,16 @@ export function getAccessToken(): string | null {
   return accessToken;
 }
 
-/** Record which identity THIS tab believes it's authenticated as. */
+/** Record which identity THIS tab believes it's authenticated as — persisted
+ *  per-tab so it survives a reload (see the file header for why). */
 export function setCurrentUserId(userId: string | null): void {
   currentUserId = userId;
+  try {
+    if (userId) sessionStorage.setItem(TAB_USER_KEY, userId);
+    else sessionStorage.removeItem(TAB_USER_KEY);
+  } catch {
+    /* ignore — in-memory value still works for this page's lifetime */
+  }
 }
 
 export const SESSION_REPLACED_EVENT = 'bukc:session-replaced';
@@ -75,7 +99,7 @@ export interface RefreshResult {
 // the same tick shares one in-flight request instead of starting its own.
 let inFlightRefresh: Promise<RefreshResult | null> | null = null;
 
-export function refreshSession(): Promise<RefreshResult | null> {
+function rawRefresh(): Promise<RefreshResult | null> {
   if (!inFlightRefresh) {
     inFlightRefresh = fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/auth/refresh`, { method: 'POST', credentials: 'include' })
       .then(async (res) => (res.ok ? (await res.json()) as RefreshResult : null))
@@ -86,15 +110,19 @@ export function refreshSession(): Promise<RefreshResult | null> {
 }
 
 /**
- * Same as refreshSession(), but for an ALREADY-established tab (i.e. this
- * tab previously knew who it was) — the transparent 401-retry path uses
- * this, not the raw refreshSession(), specifically to catch the cross-tab
- * cookie-overwrite case above. A mismatch means another login on another
- * tab silently replaced this tab's session; we surface that explicitly
- * rather than adopting the new identity.
+ * The one function anything should call to (re)establish a session — the
+ * app-load bootstrap AND the transparent 401-retry both go through this, so
+ * the cross-tab mismatch check applies uniformly everywhere a refresh can
+ * happen, not just one of the two paths. If this tab has a previously-
+ * confirmed identity (from sessionStorage, so this also catches a reload)
+ * and the refresh comes back as someone else, that's a replaced session —
+ * clear everything and signal it rather than silently adopting the new
+ * identity. A tab with no prior identity yet (genuinely first load) has
+ * nothing to compare against, so whatever the cookie says becomes this tab's
+ * starting point, same as before.
  */
-async function refreshAndCheckIdentity(): Promise<RefreshResult | null> {
-  const result = await refreshSession();
+export async function refreshSession(): Promise<RefreshResult | null> {
+  const result = await rawRefresh();
   if (result && currentUserId && result.user.userId !== currentUserId) {
     setAccessToken(null);
     setCurrentUserId(null);
@@ -106,6 +134,16 @@ async function refreshAndCheckIdentity(): Promise<RefreshResult | null> {
 
 export async function api<T = unknown>(path: string, opts: RequestOptions = {}): Promise<T> {
   const { body, _isRetry, headers, ...rest } = opts;
+  // Captured before the request fires — this is what decides whether a 401
+  // response even COULD mean "your token expired." A request made with no
+  // access token attached (login, register, forgot-password, ...) getting a
+  // 401 means "wrong credentials" or "not authorized," full stop — there is
+  // no token to have expired, so a silent refresh-and-retry here would be
+  // both meaningless and actively dangerous (it can silently adopt whatever
+  // identity happens to be sitting in the shared browser cookie from a
+  // completely unrelated tab, on a page where the user hasn't even
+  // successfully logged in yet).
+  const hadAuthHeader = Boolean(accessToken);
 
   const res = await fetch(`${API_BASE}${path}`, {
     ...rest,
@@ -118,9 +156,10 @@ export async function api<T = unknown>(path: string, opts: RequestOptions = {}):
     body: body !== undefined ? JSON.stringify(body) : undefined,
   });
 
-  // transparent refresh on expired access token
-  if (res.status === 401 && !_isRetry) {
-    const refreshed = await refreshAndCheckIdentity();
+  // transparent refresh on expired access token — only meaningful for a
+  // request that was actually authenticated to begin with
+  if (res.status === 401 && !_isRetry && hadAuthHeader) {
+    const refreshed = await refreshSession();
     if (refreshed) {
       setAccessToken(refreshed.accessToken);
       return api<T>(path, { ...opts, _isRetry: true });
-- 
2.43.0

